@metadata
title = Installation
summary = Install the command line tool, the editor extension, or a library.
@endmetadata

# Installation

Pick whichever fits how you work. Nothing here has a runtime dependency.

## Command line

The fastest way to see what the format does.

@code(language="shell")
go install github.com/SaiMouli3/xtxt/cmd/xtxt@latest
@endcode

Go installs binaries into `$(go env GOPATH)/bin`. If `xtxt` is not found
afterwards, that directory is not on your `PATH`:

@code(language="shell")
export PATH="$PATH:$(go env GOPATH)/bin"     # add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc
@endcode

Check it:

@code(language="shell")
xtxt --help
@endcode

## Editor

The VS Code extension is published as **XTXT**. Search it in the Extensions
panel, or:

@code(language="shell")
code --install-extension xtxt.xtxt
@endcode

It gives you highlighting, a live preview, problems as you type, an outline,
folding, image paste and a chart builder. See the editors page for the detail.

For Obsidian, the plugin lives in the repository under `integrations/obsidian`.

## Libraries

@table
Language | Install
---------|--------
Go | `go get github.com/SaiMouli3/xtxt`
JavaScript | `npm install xtxt-js`
Python | `pip install xtxt`
Rust | `cargo add xtxt`
Java | `io.github.saimouli3:xtxt` — built from source for now
C | `cd sdk/c && make`
C++ | `cd sdk/cpp && cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build`
@endtable

## For an agent

An MCP server lets a model read and write XTXT documents directly:

@code(language="shell")
npx xtxt-mcp
@endcode

## From source

@code(language="shell")
git clone https://github.com/SaiMouli3/xtxt
cd xtxt
go test ./...
go run ./cmd/xtxt --help
@endcode
